  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Chef is hosting a party. In preparation, he has laid out NNN glasses of juice on a table, all in a line.\n\nThe iii-th glass from the left initially has AiA_iAi​ units of juice.\n\nFor aesthetic reasons, Chef would really like all the glasses to have an equal amount of juice.\n\nTo achieve this, he can do the following:\n\n\nChoose an index iii (1≤i≤N1 \\leq i \\leq N1≤i≤N).\nChoose two **non-negative integers** xxx and yyy such that x+y≤Aix+y \\leq A_ix+y≤Ai​.\nThen,\n\nIf i&gt;1i \\gt 1i&gt;1, pour xxx units of juice into glass i−1i-1i−1, i.e. increase Ai−1A_{i-1}Ai−1​ by xxx.\nIf i&lt;Ni \\lt Ni&lt;N, pour yyy units of juice into glass i+1i+1i+1, i.e. increase Ai+1A_{i+1}Ai+1​ by yyy.\nRemove glass iii from the table. This reduces NNN by 111.\nRe-index the remaining glasses to start from 111, left-to-right.\n\n\nFind the **minimum** number of such operations that Chef needs, to ensure that all remaining glasses have an equal amount of juice remaining.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of two lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains a single integer NNN — the initial number of glasses.\nThe second line contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​ — the initial amounts of juice in the glasses.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the answer: the minimum number of operations needed to make all the glasses have an equal amount of juice.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤10001 \\leq T \\leq 10001≤T≤1000",
      "1≤N≤50001 \\leq N \\leq 50001≤N≤5000",
      "1≤Ai≤1091 \\leq A_i \\leq 10^91≤Ai​≤109",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 500050005000."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n3\n1 6 2\n4\n7 7 7 7\n5\n4 3 1 1 2",
        "output": "1\n0\n3",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: Choose i=2i = 2i=2, x=3x = 3x=3, y=2y = 2y=2 (which is valid because x+y≤A2=6x+y \\leq A_2 = 6x+y≤A2​=6).\nThis corresponds to pouring 333 units into the left glass and 222 units into the right one, and then removing the middle glass.\nAfter this, the glasses have quantities [4,4][4, 4][4,4], so we're done.\nTest case 222: All the glasses have equal quantities already, no moves are needed.\nTest case 333: One optimal sequence of operations is as follows.\n\nChoose i=3,x=1,y=0i = 3, x = 1, y = 0i=3,x=1,y=0. The glasses are now [4,4,1,2][4, 4, 1, 2][4,4,1,2].\nChoose i=3,x=0,y=1i = 3, x = 0, y = 1i=3,x=0,y=1. The glasses are now [4,4,3][4, 4, 3][4,4,3].\nChoose i=2,x=1,y=2i = 2, x = 1, y = 2i=2,x=1,y=2. The glasses are now [5,5][5, 5][5,5], and we're done.\n\nIt can be shown that using two or fewer moves is not possible."
      }
    ]
  },
